      "why": "unrolled_k with k=2-4 can achieve 1 result/cycle throughput (ceil(N/k)+3 <= 4 cycles for N<=16, k>=4 gives 7 cycles... actually k=4, N=16 gives ceil(16/4)+3=7 cycles, which is 143 Msps > 32 Msps). k=2-4 balances area (more parallel datapath) against throughput. Same accuracy parameter sweep as iterative since accuracy is identical across families."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires 32 Msps throughput, which means the CORDIC must produce at least 1 result per cycle (at Fmax >= 32 MHz). Both iterative and unrolled_k can meet this: iterative is always 1/cycle, unrolled_k with k>=4 and N<=16 gives ceil(16/4)+3=7 cycles per result, which at a modest Fmax still exceeds 32 Msps. The accuracy constraint max_abs_err <= 2^-12 requires n_iter >= 12 (standard CORDIC convergence). I focus data_width at 14-16 (minimum for 12-bit accuracy with Q1.(W-2) output) and sweep guard bits to find the cheapest configuration. The sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 us constraint with 32 requests per tick is tight: at 1 result/cycle and Fmax ~100 MHz, 32 results take 320 ns, leaving 120 ns margin. Higher Fmax (from smaller area) helps here. I split the budget evenly between the two families to map the trade-off between shared-datapath (iterative, lower area, lower Fmax) and unrolled (higher area, potentially higher Fmax) approaches."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The current families (iterative, unrolled_k) are fundamentally incapable of meeting the throughput constraint. The spec requires a throughput of 32 MSPS (32 results per microsecond, i.e., 1 result per cycle at 32 MHz or higher). The 'iterative' and 'unrolled_k' architectures produce one result every several cycles (N+3 or ceil(N/k)+3 cycles). Even with aggressive unrolling (k=8) and high Fmax, the best throughput seen was only 13.2 MSPS (iterative) and 12 MSPS (unrolled_k), which is far below the 32 MSPS requirement. Furthermore, the system constraint sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 us requires processing a burst of 32 requests within 0.44 us. A non-pipelined or partially unrolled design that takes multiple cycles per result will queue these requests, leading to latencies in the range of 2.42 us as seen in the least-violating designs. Only the 'pipelined' or 'pipelined_m' families, which produce 1 result per cycle, can achieve the necessary throughput and low batch latency. Therefore, I must explore the pipelined families to find a feasible design.",
